  "question": {
    "text": "Number of compounds with one lone pair of electrons on central atom amongst following is _<br/><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>SF</mi><mn>4</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ClF</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>NH</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>BrF</mi><mn>5</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>XeF</mi><mn>4</mn></msub></math>"
  },
  "options": [],
  "correct_answer": "4",
  "solution": {
    "text": "<p>A pair of electrons which is not shared by any of the reacting atoms is called a lone pair of electrons. <strong>Lone pairs </strong>are found in the outermost <strong>electron</strong> shell of atoms. They can be identified by using a Lewis structure.</p><p>The structures of the compounds are as follows,</p><p><img src=\"assets\\st0079_de1b2f3ea531.png\" style=\"width: 400px; height: 441px;\"/></p><p>Hence the compounds <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>NH</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>SF</mi><mn>4</mn></msub><mo> </mo><mi>and</mi><mo> </mo><msub><mi>BrF</mi><mn>5</mn></msub></math> have one lone pair on central metal atom.</p>"
  }
